00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.249 align:start position:0%
foreign policy chief Catherine Ashton  confirmed that EU member states had  finally found a united position over  events in Syria Ashton said that there  should be no military intervention  before UN inspectors released the report  on last month's chemical attack but she  said evidence suggests that Bashar al  Assad's forces are to blame in the face  of the cynical use of chemical weapons  the international community cannot  remain idle a clear and strong response  is crucial to make clear that such  crimes are unacceptable and that there  can be no impunity the agreement  convinced Germany to sign a global  statement drafted at the g20 meeting in  st. Petersburg calling for a response to  the Syrian government sources said John  Kerry had refused to give assurances  that the US would hold off on a military  strike but he said the statement was a  step forward we are very grateful for  the statement that came out of the  meeting today with respect to Syria a  strong statement about the need for  accountability and I'm particularly  grateful for the president's comments in  the earlier this morning and the foreign  ministers support for the efforts to  hold the Assad regime accountable for  what it is done the minister said that  the International Court of Justice and  The Hague should look into prosecuting  the perpetrators of the chemical attack
 

